WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
In this lesson we'll go through the reviewer queue as an operational system. We'll look at

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:11.520
how low confidence outputs get routed in, what the reviewer interface should show, how approve,

3
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:17.600
edit, and reject actions feed back into model improvement, and how to handle latency constraints

4
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:24.159
and bypass scenarios. Let's get into it. A low confidence output has to go somewhere,

5
00:00:24.159 --> 00:00:29.120
and that somewhere is a review queue. It is a dedicated work surface, separate from your

6
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.959
production pipeline, where flagged outputs land for human inspection. It isn't just a list of

7
00:00:34.959 --> 00:00:41.360
items. It is a system with priority ordering, context presentation, and action capture. The

8
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:47.680
reviewer action set is what people do once they see an item, and it is deliberately narrow. Approve

9
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:54.400
the output as correct, reject it entirely, or edit it into the correct answer. Ambiguous actions set

10
00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:00.560
slow reviewers down and produce inconsistent records. The bypass policy is the rule that lets

11
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:07.120
certain outputs skip the queue, because not every situation can wait for a reviewer. Let's look at

12
00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:13.040
the queue architecture from the top. The queue sits between the model and whatever uses its output,

13
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:19.279
whether that's an end user, a downstream system, or an automated process. When the model produces

14
00:01:19.279 --> 00:01:24.800
an output, a routing layer checks the confidence score against your stratification policy. If the

15
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:30.639
output falls below threshold or hits a high stakes classification, it goes to the queue instead of

16
00:01:30.639 --> 00:01:37.199
being released. High confidence, low stakes outputs continue downstream without interruption.

17
00:01:37.199 --> 00:01:42.480
The queue holds flagged items in a prioritized list, with the lowest confidence and highest

18
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:48.160
stakes outputs surfacing first. Items stay in the queue until a reviewer acts on them.

19
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.919
The two operational metrics you track from day one are queue depth, how many items are waiting,

20
00:01:53.919 --> 00:02:00.480
and queue latency, how long items wait before review. The reviewer interface is where the system

21
00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:06.480
either succeeds or fails in practice. If reviewers have to hunt for context, they'll make faster

22
00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.080
decisions with less information, and the quality of those decisions will suffer. A well-designed

23
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.199
interface puts everything on one screen. The original input that generated the model's output

24
00:02:17.199 --> 00:02:23.360
appears on the left or top. The model's output and its confidence score appear alongside it.

25
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:29.039
If a secondary system or rule engine has generated a suggested correction, that shows up too. The

26
00:02:29.039 --> 00:02:34.399
reviewer reads the input, evaluates the output against it, considers the suggestion, and makes

27
00:02:34.399 --> 00:02:40.160
a decision, all without navigating away. That single screen constraint keeps reviews fast

28
00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:46.080
and keeps decision quality high. Anything that forces a tab switch or a search adds friction

29
00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:52.080
and degrades throughput. Each reviewer action carries a distinct meaning in the system's record.

30
00:02:52.639 --> 00:02:58.720
Approve means the reviewer has confirmed the output is correct and it can be released downstream.

31
00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.759
The approval is logged with a timestamp and reviewer identity. Edit means the output was

32
00:03:03.759 --> 00:03:09.520
wrong or incomplete and the reviewer has provided the correct version. The edited output gets

33
00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:15.119
released and the original plus correction pair goes into a data set for future training or

34
00:03:15.119 --> 00:03:21.759
calibration. Reject means the output is wrong and no corrected version should be released. Maybe the

35
00:03:21.759 --> 00:03:26.399
input itself was ambiguous or the correct answer requires information the reviewer doesn't have.

36
00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:33.039
Reject discards the item from the release path and flags the input type for further analysis.

37
00:03:33.039 --> 00:03:38.240
All three actions close the loop differently, but all three generate a logged record.

38
00:03:39.199 --> 00:03:45.600
Four design principles separate a functional review queue from one that reviewers game or ignore.

39
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:52.479
The first is a single action surface. Reviewers should be able to approve, edit, or reject without

40
00:03:52.479 --> 00:03:58.639
opening another tool, searching a knowledge base, or switching context. Every additional step adds

41
00:03:58.639 --> 00:04:06.479
latency and introduces inconsistency. The second is context first. Input, output, and confidence score

42
00:04:07.119 --> 00:04:13.119
all need to be visible before the reviewer takes any action. Don't bury the confidence score in a

43
00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:19.359
metadata panel. Put it where reviewers see it immediately. The third is queue priority. High

44
00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:24.959
stakes and low confidence items should surface at the top of the reviewers list automatically.

45
00:04:25.519 --> 00:04:31.200
Reviewers should never have to sort their own queue. The fourth is the audit trail. Every action

46
00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:38.160
logs who reviewed the item, what changed, and when. The most underused feature of a review queue is the

47
00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:44.799
feedback loop it creates for model improvement. Every reviewer action is a labeled data point.

48
00:04:44.799 --> 00:04:49.679
When a reviewer approves an output, you've confirmed the model's behavior on that specific

49
00:04:49.679 --> 00:04:55.760
input type at that confidence level. That's a positive training signal. When a reviewer edits

50
00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:02.559
an output, you've captured the correct input-output pair. Edit corrections are high value training data

51
00:05:02.559 --> 00:05:08.399
because they come from real production inputs, not from curated data sets, and they're labeled by a

52
00:05:08.399 --> 00:05:14.640
domain expert who examined the specific failure. When a reviewer rejects an output, you've identified

53
00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:21.040
an input class the model handles poorly. Aggregated rejects point directly at the areas where the model

54
00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:26.720
needs additional training or where the input pre-processing needs rethinking. The queue pays

55
00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:32.720
for itself through the data it generates. Adding a review step creates latency, and you need to

56
00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:38.640
decide how to handle that before the system goes to production. Synchronous review means the

57
00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:44.799
downstream consumer waits for a reviewer decision before receiving the output. This is appropriate

58
00:05:44.799 --> 00:05:50.559
when the downstream process can tolerate variable response times, but it creates a hard dependency

59
00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:57.519
on reviewer availability. If no reviewer is active, the pipeline stalls. Asynchronous review returns a

60
00:05:57.519 --> 00:06:03.359
placeholder to the downstream consumer immediately and fulfills the actual output once a review

61
00:06:03.359 --> 00:06:08.799
decision is made. This keeps the pipeline moving, but requires the downstream system to handle

62
00:06:08.799 --> 00:06:15.200
deferred fulfillment. Some use cases work well with async, others don't. A medical documentation

63
00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:20.480
system that needs the correct output before a clinician signs off can't use a placeholder.

64
00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:27.040
Design the latency model explicitly and early before downstream integrations are built around

65
00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:33.200
assumptions. The bypass policy handles the cases where waiting for a reviewer isn't possible.

66
00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:38.320
There are three main scenarios where bypass is appropriate. Emergency override applies when a

67
00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:43.920
time-critical situation means that review delay causes more harm than the risk of an unreviewed

68
00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:50.320
output. This should be rare and tightly defined. A high confidence floor bypass allows outputs above

69
00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:56.320
a validated confidence floor to skip the queue automatically. The threshold has to be validated

70
00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:02.959
empirically, not set arbitrarily, and every bypass should still be logged. The third scenario is

71
00:07:02.959 --> 00:07:08.399
reviewer unavailability. If the queue has no active reviewers and the business needs continuity,

72
00:07:08.399 --> 00:07:14.000
a fallback rule can release outputs with a warning flag. In all three cases, the bypass

73
00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:20.000
action gets logged with the same completeness as a normal review decision. Bypass isn't an escape

74
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:26.160
from accountability. A review queue is the infrastructure that makes human oversight

75
00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:33.119
scalable, auditable, and genuinely useful for model improvement. Routing, the reviewer interface,

76
00:07:33.119 --> 00:07:38.880
the action set, and the bypass policy all have to work together for the system to hold up under

77
00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:44.799
production load. Get any of these wrong and the queue becomes a delay rather than a quality

78
00:07:44.799 --> 00:07:49.040
control. That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

